# Optional. The connector normally gets its session from a browser profile it
# owns — see "Credentials" in the README — and needs none of this.
#
# Use this file to run somewhere without a browser: CI, a container, a server.
# Copy to .env, fill in, then: chmod 600 .env
#
# Your Legal One tenant. No default is baked into the source — an unset value
# raises a named error rather than quietly pointing at someone else's install.
LEGALONE_BASE_URL=https://<your-tenant>.novajus.com.br

# The whole Cookie request header from an authenticated browser session:
# DevTools → Network → any request to your tenant → copy "Cookie".
#
# There is no anti-forgery token, so this cookie is the entire credential — full
# authority over the account, not scoped to timesheets. It dies with the browser
# session. Past experimentation, keep it in the keychain and read it at runtime.
LEGALONE_COOKIE="cookie_login_method=…; .ASPXAUTH=…; …"

# Optional. Where the browser profile that holds the single sign-on session lives.
# Defaults to the per-OS application directory (see "Credentials" in the README).
# Set it to keep the profile on an encrypted volume, to run two tenants side by
# side, or to exercise a cold start without moving HOME.
#LEGALONE_PROFILE_DIR=/path/to/a/writable/dir

# Optional. Where exported .xlsx reports are written. Defaults to an `exports`
# directory next to the profile.
#LEGALONE_EXPORT_DIR=/path/to/exports
